        <article class="in037-b03 in037-b03--guide">
          <h1>Migraine: symptoms, triggers and when to get help</h1>
          <p class="in037-b03__lead">Migraine is a common health condition. Attacks can differ from person to person, so recognising your own pattern can help when you speak with a doctor.</p>

          <h2>Common features</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>A throbbing headache, often on one side of the head.</li>
            <li>Feeling sick or being sick.</li>
            <li>Sensitivity to light or sound.</li>
            <li>For some people, warning signs such as visual changes before the headache. This is called an aura.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Keep a simple headache diary</h2>
          <p>Note when each attack starts, how long it lasts, the symptoms you notice and anything that may be part of your pattern, such as sleep, meals or stress. Take the diary to your appointment.</p>

          <h2>Arrange a medical appointment</h2>
          <p>Speak with a doctor if attacks are frequent, severe, getting worse, changing from your usual pattern or affecting daily life.</p>

          <h2 class="in037-b03__urgent-title">Get emergency help immediately</h2>
          <div class="in037-b03__urgent">
            <p>Use local emergency services or go to the nearest hospital for a sudden, extremely painful headache, or a headache with problems speaking, remembering or seeing; confusion or drowsiness; a seizure; weakness on one side; a very high temperature with meningitis symptoms; or after a recent head injury.</p>
          </div>

          <p class="in037-b03__actions"><a href="/pages/online-consultation">Online consultation information</a><a href="/pages/contact-us">Contact Kairali India</a></p>
          <p class="in037-b03__source">Source: <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/Migraine/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">NHS, Migraine</a>. General information only; use local Indian healthcare and emergency services.</p>
        </article>

      {%- when 'postpregnancy' -%}
        <article class="in037-b03 in037-b03--guide">
          <h1>After childbirth: recovery, check-ups and warning signs</h1>
          <p class="in037-b03__lead">The weeks after birth are a recovery period for both physical and emotional health. Regular postnatal care during the first six weeks supports you and your baby.</p>

          <h2>What postnatal care covers</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Follow-up contact with a qualified midwife, nurse or doctor.</li>
            <li>Checks for you and your baby.</li>
            <li>Support with feeding and physical recovery.</li>
            <li>Attention to emotional wellbeing.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>If follow-up has not been arranged, ask your local health service when and where your postnatal check will happen.</p>

          <h2>Recovery is individual</h2>
          <p>Bleeding, soreness and tiredness can occur in the early weeks, but recovery does not follow one timetable. Raise any concern with your midwife, nurse or doctor. If low mood, anxiety or distress continues or gets worse, tell a health professional.</p>

          <h2 class="in037-b03__urgent-title">Get urgent medical help immediately</h2>
          <div class="in037-b03__urgent">
            <p>Go to the nearest hospital or use local emergency services if bleeding becomes sudden or very heavy, especially with faintness or a fast heartbeat, or if you believe something is seriously wrong.</p>
          </div>

          <p class="in037-b03__actions"><a href="/pages/online-consultation">Online consultation information</a><a href="/pages/contact-us">Contact Kairali India</a></p>
          <p class="in037-b03__source">Sources: <a href="https://www.who.int/activities/raising-the-importance-of-postnatal-care" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">WHO, Postnatal care</a>; <a href="https://www.who.int/tools/your-life-your-health/life-phase/pregnancy--birth-and-after-childbirth/getting-the-health-services-you-need-after-childbirth" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">WHO, Health services after childbirth</a>; <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/pregnancy/labour-and-birth/your-body/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">NHS, Your body after the birth</a>. General information only; use local Indian healthcare and emergency services.</p>
        </article>

      {%- when 'premature-ejaculation' -%}
